Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/mediacodec: present decode-order container timestamps in presentation order

An MP4 that stores B-frame H.264 or HEVC without composition offsets (no
ctts box: a raw stream remuxed with generated timestamps, as in the RARBG
x265 releases) gives every access unit its decode-order time. MediaCodec
echoes each frame's queued timestamp back with the frame, so in presentation
order the timestamps run backwards on every reordered frame. A libavcodec
decoder recovers because its frames also carry the packet DTS and mpv falls
back to it; MediaCodec frames carry none, so mpv dropped most frames as late:
on a Pixel 7 such a film rendered at 9 fps and drifted 38 s from its audio in
two minutes, while software decoding played it cleanly.

Watch for exactly that stream: queued timestamps strictly increasing in
decode order while the output steps backwards. Once seen, hand each frame the
queued timestamps in increasing order, which is what VLC does for these files
(androidx/media#3347 has the same failure on Media3). Frames that will never be
output are taken out of that timeline so the frames after them keep their
times: HEVC RASL pictures that follow the CRA decoding started from are known
not to be output when they are queued (and counted back in if a decoder
outputs them anyway), and any other frame is written off once more frames that
precede it in decode order are missing than the stream's reorder bound allows.
A stream that ever queues a timestamp at or below its predecessor orders its
own timestamps, or is discontinuous, and is left alone for the session, as is
one without timestamps. Only H.264, HEVC, MPEG-2 and MPEG-4 video are watched.

The recovered time replaces frame->pts only. buffer->pts, which the
rendered-frame feedback matches against the codec's reports, keeps the codec's
own timestamp.
